      'The ear has two tasks: hearing, and sensing the position and movement of the head for balance. It has three parts, which work in sequence: the external ear collects sound, the middle ear amplifies it and the inner ear turns it into nerve signals. Most of the ear lies within the temporal bone, which protects its delicate parts.',
      'The external ear consists of the auricle, the visible part, and the ear canal. The auricle is a sheet of elastic cartilage covered with skin, shaped with folds and hollows that collect sound and help us tell where it comes from. Only the earlobe has no cartilage. The skin is attached tightly to the cartilage, so a blow to the ear can cause bleeding between them, and if this is not drained, the cartilage can die and leave a deformed "cauliflower ear".',
