      'The middle ear is a small air-filled space in the temporal bone, between the eardrum and the inner ear. It contains a chain of three tiny bones, the ossicles: the malleus (hammer), incus (anvil) and stapes (stirrup), which carry vibrations from the eardrum to the inner ear. Because the eardrum is much larger than the oval window and the ossicles act as a lever, sound is concentrated and amplified, which is necessary because the inner ear is filled with fluid, which is harder to set vibrating than air.',
